--TEST--
`new $c()` and `$c::f()` take a written class name, as `$x is $c` already does (`rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A class name is written, never computed — the same line `$$var` and `eval`
// are already on. Nothing below the checker could resolve one either, since
// `nvs-codegen` bakes a descriptor's address in as a constant, so all three
// spellings that reach a class through a value are one mistake under one
// code: `new $c()`, `$c::f()` and the value arm of an `is` test.
final class Box {
    public static function make(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

string $className = "Box";
mixed $o = new $className();
mixed $p = $className::make();
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0496]: the target of `new` must be a written class name
%A
error[E0496]: the class side of a `::` call must be a written class name
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
